{"content":[{"c":["BITHRON*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Term in ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.2.29","r":"2Sam.2.29","rawR":"2Sam.2.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.2.29","value":"2Sam.2.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Samuel 2:29"}," (",{"c":["kjv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},"; ",{"c":["nlt"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"}," mg) whose meaning is uncertain. Abner, commander of Ishbosheth’s army, fled through Bithron after losing a battle to David’s army. The Hebrew root has the meaning “to cut into pieces.” Three explanations have been suggested: (1) it refers to a valley, perhaps the Jabbok; (2) it is the area “cut off” by a great curve in the Jabbok River; (3) it refers to the first part of the day, “all through the morning” (",{"c":["nlt"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Bithron","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"2 Samuel 2:29","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.2.29","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.2.29","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.2.29"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c4ace67a8f1ca3ed50fcc2ecb3e44e559a53587e843184914489932683807538","sourceItemName":"Bithron","sourceItemOrder":332,"sourceMember":"Articles/B.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Bithron","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Bithron*","title":"Bithron*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}